# Canvas 2D Performance Optimization Playbook

Apply these techniques when a Canvas 2D game (or any procedural-drawing app) needs to render hundreds of objects at 60 FPS.

## 1. Sprite Cache — Flyweight Pattern

Pre-render each unique visual into an off-screen canvas **once**, then blit with `drawImage`.

- **Cache key** = `itemId + sizeBase + palette` — every combination that changes appearance.
- **Render scale**: draw at 4× device size into the cache for crisp results at any zoom.
- **CRITICAL**: use `document.createElement('canvas')` — **not** `OffscreenCanvas`. `OffscreenCanvas.getContext('2d')` returns `OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D`, which does NOT share the `CanvasRenderingContext2D` prototype chain. Any code that patches the context via `Object.defineProperty(ctx, 'fillStyle', …)` using a descriptor from `CanvasRenderingContext2D.prototype` will throw on an `OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D`. Regular `HTMLCanvasElement` contexts are true `CanvasRenderingContext2D` instances and work everywhere.
- Invalidate the cache when the active palette/world changes (`setSpriteGeneration(key)`).

```typescript
const el = document.createElement('canvas');
el.width = pxSize; el.height = pxSize;
const ctx = el.getContext('2d')!;        // real CanvasRenderingContext2D
ctx.scale(RENDER_SCALE, RENDER_SCALE);
ctx.translate(halfSize, halfSize);
itemDef.draw(ctx, sizeBase, palette);    // runs once per unique visual
```

At render time, a single `ctx.drawImage(cachedCanvas, …)` replaces dozens of path/gradient/fill calls per object.

## 2. Viewport Frustum Culling

Skip every object whose world-space position falls outside the camera rect + a margin:

```typescript
const halfW = canvas.width / 2 / zoom;
const halfH = canvas.height / 2 / zoom;
const margin = 80 * blobScale;
items.forEach(item => {
  if (item.x < camX - halfW - margin || item.x > camX + halfW + margin ||
      item.y < camY - halfH - margin || item.y > camY + halfH + margin) return;
  // …draw
});
```

## 3. Eliminate `shadowBlur`

`ctx.shadowBlur` is the single most expensive Canvas 2D property. Replace with a radial-gradient "halo" drawn before the item:

```typescript
const shR = size * 0.55;
const shGrad = ctx.createRadialGradient(0, size * 0.03, size * 0.15, 0, size * 0.03, shR);
shGrad.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.14)');
shGrad.addColorStop(0.6, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.04)');
shGrad.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
ctx.fillStyle = shGrad;
ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(0, size * 0.03, shR, 0, Math.PI * 2); ctx.fill();
```

Remove **every** `ctx.shadowBlur`, `ctx.shadowColor`, `ctx.shadowOffsetX/Y` assignment from the hot path.

## 4. Batch Identical Draw Calls

Merge many small identical shapes (grid dots, ambient particles) into **one** `beginPath` → loop of `moveTo/arc` → **one** `fill`:

```typescript
ctx.fillStyle = color;
ctx.beginPath();
for (const p of points) {
  ctx.moveTo(p.x + r, p.y);
  ctx.arc(p.x, p.y, r, 0, Math.PI * 2);
}
ctx.fill();  // single GPU dispatch for all dots
```

## 5. `setTransform` Instead of `save/restore`

For simple items (translate + rotate), skip `save()/restore()` and compute the affine matrix directly:

```typescript
const cos = Math.cos(rot), sin = Math.sin(rot);
ctx.setTransform(
  zoom * cos,  zoom * sin,
  -zoom * sin, zoom * cos,
  (item.x - camX) * zoom + screenCx,
  (item.y - camY) * zoom + screenCy
);
drawCachedItem(ctx, …);
```

After the item loop, restore with `ctx.setTransform(1,0,0,1,0,0)`. Reserve `save()/restore()` only for items that modify `globalAlpha`, `clip`, or `setLineDash`.

## 6. Reuse `Path2D` Objects

Build complex paths once at module scope and reuse:

```typescript
const STAR_PATH = new Path2D();
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) { /* build star vertices */ }
// in draw: ctx.fill(STAR_PATH); ctx.stroke(STAR_PATH);
```

## 7. Cache Color Computations and Gradient Stops

`darken()`, `lighten()`, and HSL parsing are expensive when called thousands of times per frame. Memoize with a `Map<string, result>`:

```typescript
const _darkenCache = new Map<string, string>();
export function darken(hex: string, amount = 0.25): string {
  const key = `${hex}|${amount}`;
  let v = _darkenCache.get(key);
  if (!v) { v = computeDarken(hex, amount); _darkenCache.set(key, v); }
  return v;
}
```

Also cache **gradient color-stop arrays** keyed by base color so the HSL math runs once per unique color, not once per gradient creation.

## 8. Single `requestAnimationFrame` Loop

Merge game-tick logic and rendering into one `rAF` callback. Two separate `rAF` loops cause double scheduling overhead and can drift out of sync.

## 9. High-Frequency Data Outside the State Store

Never store per-frame mutable arrays (e.g. frame timestamps for FPS tracking) inside Zustand/Redux state. Each `set()` creates a new object, triggers subscriber diffing, and causes React re-renders.

Instead, keep a module-level mutable array and expose a getter:

```typescript
let _frameTimes: number[] = [];
export function getFrameTimes() { return _frameTimes; }
// in tick: _frameTimes.push(performance.now());
// in UI: poll with setInterval(fn, 250) instead of subscribing to store
```

## 10. World-Space vs. Pixel-Space

When spawning or bounding objects programmatically (benchmarks, procedural levels), always convert screen pixels to world coordinates:

```typescript
const worldHalfW = (window.innerWidth / zoom) * 0.45;
const worldHalfH = (window.innerHeight / zoom) * 0.45;
```

The camera applies `zoom = baseZoom / blobScale`. Spawning in pixel coords will scatter objects far outside the visible area.

## Priority Order

Apply in this order for maximum impact per effort:

1. Sprite cache (flyweight) — eliminates 90%+ of per-frame draw calls
2. Remove `shadowBlur` — single biggest per-pixel cost
3. Viewport culling — skip invisible objects entirely
4. Batch small shapes — reduces state-change overhead
5. `setTransform` fast path — avoids canvas state stack
6. Cache colors / gradient stops — reduces CPU allocation
7. Merge rAF loops — reduces scheduling overhead
8. `Path2D` reuse — minor but free
